   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT relating to the collection of property taxes and interest for the year 2015",
    "basePrintNo": "S5876",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5876-2015",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "",
    "lawSection": "Real Property Taxation",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S5876\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act relating to the collection of property taxes and interest for\nthe year 2015\n\nPURPOSE:\n\nThis legislation provides property tax payers with a one-day\nextension, without penalty, to make payments in light of the snow\nstorm that hit much of New York State on the original due date.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill grants real property tax payers a one-day\nextension, pursuant to the adoption of a local resolution, for the\nremittance of property taxes that would have otherwise been due on\nFebruary 2, 2015, without penalty or interest, to February 3, 2015.\n\nSection 2 of the bill states this act shall take effect immediately.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIn New York State, real property taxes are allowed to be paid without\na charge for interest on or before January 31 each year. In 2015,\nJanuary 31 fell on a Saturday and property taxpayers were given until\nthe next business day, Monday February 2, to pay such taxes. However,\non February 2, 2015 a major storm blanketed much of the state with\nsignificant snowfalls and icy conditions that prevented many property\ntaxpayers from safely leaving their homes to pay such taxes. This\nlegislation would give affected parties an additional day to pay their\nproperty tax bill without incurring interest charges.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone to the State, a small reduction in interest payments to local\ngovernments, but for a reason that preserves neighborly treatment\nbetween local governments and their residents.\n\nIMPACT ON REGULATION OF BUSINESSES AND INDIVIDUALS:\n\nThis act would prevent businesses and property owners from having to\npay fines and interest for late payments occurring as a result of\ncircumstances out of their control.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n\n",
